From 2b75ce90705674c38949fee98d1822ccafd07ed7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 13:04:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] accept a promoted unsigned char or short for %u and
 %x

With -e-acsl-validate-format-strings, the instrumentation describes each
variadic argument of printf and friends by its type, and the runtime
requires that type to equal the one the directive expects. An argument of
type unsigned char, unsigned short or _Bool reaches the instrumentation
promoted to int, so it was described as int, and formatting a byte with
"%u" or "%x" aborted:

  snprintf: directive 1 ('%u') expects argument of type 'unsigned int'
  but the corresponding argument has type 'int'

Every value of these types is representable in both int and unsigned int
(C11 7.16.1.1p2), and "%hhu" already accepted the same argument.

The instrumentation (Functions.Libc.get_printf_argument_str) now looks
through the promotion to int and describes such an argument by its own
type: 'C' for unsigned char (also used for _Bool, and for char where char
is unsigned), 'H' for unsigned short. The runtime accepts these where it
expects int or unsigned int, and names them by their own type otherwise.
Signed types smaller than int are still described as int: a negative value
is not representable in unsigned int. int and unsigned int stay distinct,
so the existing printf.c test is unchanged.

Regression test: tests/format/printf_promoted.c.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
